OSKit Arch — aarch64 POSIX layout helpers
==========================================

The POSIX shim writes Linux-shaped structs (`struct kstat`,
`struct timespec`, etc.) into user buffers. Most of those have
identical wire layouts across the arches we care about, so they
live in `oskit.posix` directly. A handful — `struct kstat`
chief among them — are arch-specific because slix-musl's per-
arch kstat header reorders fields to match the arch's Linux
ABI. Those packers live here, exported through `module oskit.arch`
so the shim can `import oskit.arch.{pack_kstat}` without caring
which arch it's compiled against.

aarch64-slix `struct kstat` mirrors the arm64 Linux kernel
layout. Crucially, slix-musl's `arch/aarch64-slix/bits/alltypes.h.in`
overrides the generic `nlink_t = unsigned _Reg` and
`blksize_t = long` to plain 4-byte `unsigned int` / `int` — so
the struct ends up at exactly 128 bytes, not 144 like x86_64.
This was caught the hard way: a 144-byte writer overwrote
`__fstatat`'s saved LR at sp+160 and the program crashed
returning to PC=0. Don't change KSTAT_SIZE without a matching
disassembly check.

Field offsets (verified by hand against `arch/aarch64-slix/`
overrides + the generic kstat header — keep in sync if the
musl header ever moves):

```
  0  st_dev          (u64)
  8  st_ino          (u64)
 16  st_mode         (u32)
 20  st_nlink        (u32)
 24  st_uid          (u32)
 28  st_gid          (u32)
 32  st_rdev         (u64)
 40  __pad           (u64)
 48  st_size         (i64)
 56  st_blksize      (i32)
 60  __pad2          (i32)
 64  st_blocks       (i64)
 72  st_atime        (i64)
 80  st_atime_nsec   (i64)
 88  st_mtime        (i64)
 96  st_mtime_nsec   (i64)
104  st_ctime        (i64)
112  st_ctime_nsec   (i64)
120  __unused[2]     (u32×2 → 8 B)
```

All values are little-endian (aarch64 user processes run LE).

    module oskit.arch

    val KSTAT_SIZE = 128

A small write-LE helper lives here rather than borrowing the
shim's because importing oskit.posix from oskit.arch would
introduce a module cycle. The cost is ~10 lines duplicated.

    posix_write_le_i64(p: *byte, off: int, v: i64)
        var i = 0
        while i < 8
            p[off + i] = byte(int((v >> i64(8 * i))) & 0xff)
            i += 1

    posix_write_le_u32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte(v & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)

Pack the universal stat fields into the 128-byte aarch64-slix
kstat. `mode` is already in Linux musl form (S_IFREG=0o100000
etc., NOT TFS-encoded). `size` / `ino` are i64-wide so a
4 GB+ file or a >32-bit inode space remains representable; TFS
itself caps both at i32 today, but the packer stays future-
proof.

    pack_kstat(buf: *byte, mode: int, nlinks: int, uid: int, gid: int,
               size: i64, ino: i64)
        var i = 0
        while i < KSTAT_SIZE
            buf[i] = byte(0)
            i += 1
        posix_write_le_i64(buf, 0, i64(0))         // st_dev
        posix_write_le_i64(buf, 8, ino)            // st_ino
        posix_write_le_u32(buf, 16, mode)          // st_mode
        posix_write_le_u32(buf, 20, nlinks)        // st_nlink
        posix_write_le_u32(buf, 24, uid)           // st_uid
        posix_write_le_u32(buf, 28, gid)           // st_gid
        posix_write_le_i64(buf, 32, i64(0))        // st_rdev
        posix_write_le_i64(buf, 48, size)          // st_size
        posix_write_le_u32(buf, 56, 4096)          // st_blksize
        // st_blocks is a count of 512-byte units; round up.
        val blocks = (size + i64(511)) >> i64(9)
        posix_write_le_i64(buf, 64, blocks)
        // atime/mtime/ctime + nsec all stay zero — slix has no
        // wall clock for files yet.
